sections.body_md:
A **cash flow statement** reports the cash inflows and outflows of an enterprise for a period, grouped as **operating**, **investing** and **financing** activities, and ends by reconciling opening and closing **cash and cash equivalents**. It is governed by **Accounting Standard 3 (Cash Flow Statements)**. Under the Companies Act, 2013 it forms part of the financial statements of a company (a one person company, a small company and a dormant company may omit it).

**Why profit and cash differ:** profit is measured on the accrual basis (credit sales, unpaid expenses, depreciation), whereas cash changes only when money is received or paid; buying assets and repaying loans use cash without touching profit.

**Objectives:**
- to report where cash came from and where it went, by activity;
- to judge whether the main business generates enough cash to pay its way, and to judge liquidity;
- to explain the difference between profit and the change in cash;
- to help plan future cash requirements, repayments and dividends;
- to compare years and enterprises on a basis little affected by accounting policies.
